 an income gap?</li><li><span>02</span>How quickly can you reach this money?</li><li><span>03</span>What is your plan to replenish it?</li></ol></section><section class="guide-section" id="section-1"><h2>Start with your own pressure points</h2><p>List the expenses that would continue if income paused: housing, groceries, utilities, insurance, and required debt payments. Then consider the events most likely to create a shortfall, such as a repair or an unexpected bill. This gives the fund a purpose before it gives it a number.</p></section><section class="guide-section" id="section-2"><h2>Make the first milestone reachable</h2><p>Choose an amount you can build consistently, even if it is smaller than your eventual target. A recurring transfer can make progress less dependent on remembering to move money. Review the transfer when income or essential expenses change.</p></section><section class="guide-section" id="section-3"><h2>Keep access and safety in view</h2><p>The reserve should be available when an unplanned expense arrives. Compare account access, fees, and applicable deposit protection before choosing where to hold it. Review the balance after using the fund and decide how you will rebuild it.</p></section><section class="article-source" aria-labelledby="source-title"><span class="eyebrow">SOURCE &amp; FURTHER READING</span><h2 id="source-title">Check the primary source</h2><p>The guide is newly written for this preview and draws its factual background from <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/an-essential-guide-to-building-an-emergency-fund/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: An essential guide to building an emergency fund ↗</a>. Check that source and current product terms before making a decision.</p></section><div class="disclosure"><strong>Educational preview.</strong> This draft is general information for U.S. readers, not personal financial, investment, tax, or legal advice. It needs final editorial and 